  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 305 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and humid conditions with heat indices around \n105F expected through the rest of the week. Heat Advisories remain \nin effect for inland areas today and have been expanded to included \nthe remainder of the forecast area (excluding the MD and NC beaches) \nfor tomorrow. \n\nHigh pressure remains stretched across most of the area this \nafternoon, leading to mostly clear to partly cloudy skies. The only \narea not encased in the sweltering heat is the coastal areas and the \nEastern Shore. The coast is receiving some relief due to the onshore \nflow, while the Eastern Shore is seeing a combination of the onshore \nflow and a backdoor front that has buckled across that area. The MD \nEastern Shore counties have had low cloud cover that has kept them \nin the lower 80s for most of the afternoon. The inland portion of \nthe forecast area is currently observing temperatures in the 90s to \nnear 100F at some sites, with heat indices in the lower to mid \n100s. Unfortunately, this early September heat wave will \ncontinue through the end of the week as the aforementioned \nbackdoor front will lift back to the north and winds will shift \nto the S-SW tomorrow. The ridge aloft will also remain intact \nacross the Eastern U.S., and Thursday looks to be the hottest \nday of the week. Temperatures across our entire forecast area \nlook reach the upper 90s to near 100F (lower to mid 90s across \nthe Eastern Shore). With dew points in the lower to mid 70s \nforecast for tomorrow, most areas will see heat indices reaching\nHeat Advisory criteria of 105-108F, so have expanded the \ncurrent Advisory to include all but the MD and NC beaches \ntomorrow. Looking ahead to Friday, the southern half of the area\nwill likely need Heat Advisories, but how far north they will \nbe needed will be dependent on the timing of a stronger frontal \nboundary that will be approaching the region on Friday. As this \nstronger front moves through the area late Friday through \nSaturday, we will finally see a much needed break in the heat. \nBy Sunday, temperatures will be in the lower 80s, with \nrefreshing 60s dew points. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There are daily chances for thunderstorms through \nSaturday. Some storms may be strong to severe with damaging winds \ngusts and large hail. \n\nA line of thunderstorms is currently moving through the MD Eastern \nShore counties this afternoon. This convection was spawned by an MCS \nnear the eastern Great Lakes early this morning and has been riding \nalong a boundary all the way to our area. With good shear to our \nnorth and abundant daytime heating, this line has remained strong as \nit moved SE-S, bringing strong winds. Hi-res guidance had a \ndifficult time with this line earlier today, and a similar pattern \nis expected tomorrow, so we will likely be heading into tomorrow's \npossible severe weather with lower confidence in location and \ncoverage. As of right now, some hi-res guidance is suggesting more \nwidespread coverage, which is in-line with SPC's thinking since they \nhave a majority of our area in a Slight Risk (level 2/5) for severe \nweather tomorrow. Granted, this severe threat is highly contingent \non if convection does develop. There will be ample DCAPE and flow \naloft will remain NW, so there are multiple factors in favor of \nstronger convection, but even with all these good parameters, \nconfidence still remains on the lower side. The most favorable \noverlap of forcing, shear, and instability is expected Friday. At \nthe surface, the aforementioned front to our north will finally make \nappreciable progress and descend into our local area. Aloft, NW flow \nwill remain in place and progressively strengthen as the associated \ntrough drops southward along the NE/Mid-Atlantic coast, increasing \nthe deep-layer shear. This all suggests a bonafide severe wx threat \nfor the majority of the area Friday, with the potential for several \nfast-moving clusters capable of widespread damaging winds, along \nwith isolated supercells and large hail. The risk for storms (and \nany potential severe weather) Saturday is dependent on where the \nfront is situated during peak heating. After the front moves \nthrough, the risk for severe weather becomes negligible as a much \ndrier airmass filters in across the region through at least early \nnext week.",
